  "worked_solution": "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the numbers in turn.\n\n- $12$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $2$ is a factor of $12:$ \n\n$$\n12=2\\times 6\n$$\n\n- $13$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $13.$\n\n- $19$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $19.$\n\n- $23$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $23.$\n\n- $29$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $29.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$12$ only.\"",
